NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router uses a nested route tree to match up the URL with the correct component tree to render."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To build a route tree, TanStack Router supports:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./file-based-routing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File-Based Routing"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./code-based-routing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code-Based Routing"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both methods support the exact same core features and functionality, but "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"file-based routing requires less code for the same or better results"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For this reason, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"file-based routing is the preferred and recommended way"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to configure TanStack Router. Most of the documentation is written from the perspective of file-based routing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"route-trees","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Route Trees"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested routing is a powerful concept that allows you to use a URL to render a nested component tree. For example, given the URL of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/blog/posts/123"},{"type":"text","value":", you could create a route hierarchy that looks like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"├── blog\n│   ├── posts\n│   │   ├── $postId","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And render a component tree that looks like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Blog>\n  <Posts>\n    <Post postId=\"123\" />\n  </Posts>\n</Blog>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let's take that concept and expand it out to a larger site structure, but with file-names now:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── about.tsx\n├── posts/\n│   ├── index.tsx\n│   ├── $postId.tsx\n├── posts.$postId.edit.tsx\n├── settings/\n│   ├── profile.tsx\n│   ├── notifications.tsx\n├── _pathlessLayout/\n│   ├── route-a.tsx\n├── ├── route-b.tsx\n├── files/\n│   ├── $.tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The above is a valid route tree configuration that can be used with TanStack Router! There's a lot of power and convention to unpack with file-based routing, so let's break it down a bit."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"route-tree-configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Route Tree Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Route trees can be configured using a few different ways:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./file-based-routing.md#flat-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat Routes"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./file-based-routing.md#directory-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Directories"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./file-based-routing.md#mixed-flat-and-directory-routes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mixed Flat Routes and Directories"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./virtual-file-routes.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Virtual File Routes"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./code-based-routing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code-Based Routes"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Please be sure to check out the full documentation links above for each type of route tree, or just proceed to the next section to get started with file-based routing."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"route-trees","text":"Route Trees","level":2},{"id":"route-tree-configuration","text":"Route Tree Configuration","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Router uses a nested route tree to match up the URL with the correct component tree to render.\n\nTo build a route tree, TanStack Router supports:\n\n- [File-Based Routing](./file-based-routing.md)\n- [Code-Based Routing](./code-based-routing.md)\n\nBoth methods support the exact same core features and functionality, but **file-based routing requires less code for the same or better results**. For this reason, **file-based routing is the preferred and recommended way** to configure TanStack Router. Most of the documentation is written from the perspective of file-based routing.\n\n## Route Trees\n\nNested routing is a powerful concept that allows you to use a URL to render a nested component tree. For example, given the URL of `/blog/posts/123`, you could create a route hierarchy that looks like this:\n\n```tsx\n├── blog\n│   ├── posts\n│   │   ├── $postId\n```\n\nAnd render a component tree that looks like this:\n\n```tsx\n<Blog>\n  <Posts>\n    <Post postId=\"123\" />\n  </Posts>\n</Blog>\n```\n\nLet's take that concept and expand it out to a larger site structure, but with file-names now:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── about.tsx\n├── posts/\n│   ├── index.tsx\n│   ├── $postId.tsx\n├── posts.$postId.edit.tsx\n├── settings/\n│   ├── profile.tsx\n│   ├── notifications.tsx\n├── _pathlessLayout/\n│   ├── route-a.tsx\n├── ├── route-b.tsx\n├── files/\n│   ├── $.tsx\n```\n\nThe above is a valid route tree configuration that can be used with TanStack Router! There's a lot of power and convention to unpack with file-based routing, so let's break it down a bit.\n\n## Route Tree Configuration\n\nRoute trees can be configured using a few different ways:\n\n- [Flat Routes](./file-based-routing.md#flat-routes)\n- [Directories](./file-based-routing.md#directory-routes)\n- [Mixed Flat Routes and Directories](./file-based-routing.md#mixed-flat-and-directory-routes)\n- [Virtual File Routes](./virtual-file-routes.md)\n- [Code-Based Routes](./code-based-routing.md)\n\nPlease be sure to check out the full documentation links above for each type of route tree, or just proceed to the next section to get started with file-based routing.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Route Trees\n---\n\nTanStack Router uses a nested route tree to match up the URL with the correct component tree to render.\n\nTo build a route tree, TanStack Router supports:\n\n- [File-Based Routing](./file-based-routing.md)\n- [Code-Based Routing](./code-based-routing.md)\n\nBoth methods support the exact same core features and functionality, but **file-based routing requires less code for the same or better results**. For this reason, **file-based routing is the preferred and recommended way** to configure TanStack Router. Most of the documentation is written from the perspective of file-based routing.\n\n## Route Trees\n\nNested routing is a powerful concept that allows you to use a URL to render a nested component tree. For example, given the URL of `/blog/posts/123`, you could create a route hierarchy that looks like this:\n\n```tsx\n├── blog\n│   ├── posts\n│   │   ├── $postId\n```\n\nAnd render a component tree that looks like this:\n\n```tsx\n<Blog>\n  <Posts>\n    <Post postId=\"123\" />\n  </Posts>\n</Blog>\n```\n\nLet's take that concept and expand it out to a larger site structure, but with file-names now:\n\n```\n/routes\n├── __root.tsx\n├── index.tsx\n├── about.tsx\n├── posts/\n│   ├── index.tsx\n│   ├── $postId.tsx\n├── posts.$postId.edit.tsx\n├── settings/\n│   ├── profile.tsx\n│   ├── notifications.tsx\n├── _pathlessLayout/\n│   ├── route-a.tsx\n├── ├── route-b.tsx\n├── files/\n│   ├── $.tsx\n```\n\nThe above is a valid route tree configuration that can be used with TanStack Router! There's a lot of power and convention to unpack with file-based routing, so let's break it down a bit.\n\n## Route Tree Configuration\n\nRoute trees can be configured using a few different ways:\n\n- [Flat Routes](./file-based-routing.md#flat-routes)\n- [Directories](./file-based-routing.md#directory-routes)\n- [Mixed Flat Routes and Directories](./file-based-routing.md#mixed-flat-and-directory-routes)\n- [Virtual File Routes](./virtual-file-routes.md)\n- [Code-Based Routes](./code-based-routing.md)\n\nPlease be sure to check out the full documentation links above for each type of route tree, or just proceed to the next section to get started with file-based routing.\n"}
